  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...While the 10Z sounding at the Cape showed PW \nvalues around 1.6 inches this morning, GOES-19 TPW imagery shows \ndeeper moisture (PW around 2 inches) has been overspreading the area \nfrom the east with a weak inverted sfc trough. Deepest moisture has \nbeen concentrated across southern areas of east central Florida, \nwith drier air still nudging its way in across Lake/Volusia \ncounties. Therefore, greatest shower and storm coverage will \ncontinue to remain focused across the Treasure Coast, mostly inland \nthrough Okeechobee County and southern Osceola County. However, \ncan't rule out isolated/scattered showers and a storm or two farther \nnorth through the Orlando metro area and the Cape this afternoon. \nMain storm threats from any isolated stronger storms will be gusty \nwinds up to 40-45 mph, frequent lightning and locally heavy rainfall \nof 1-2 inches. This activity will spread west of the area into the \nevening, but lingering onshore flow may still allow for isolated \nonshore moving showers/storms along the Treasure Coast tonight. Lows \nwill continue to range from the mid to upper 70s for most locations. \n\nSaturday-Sunday...Atlantic ridge axis currently north of the area, \nwill shift south of the region into this weekend. This will lead to \na developing offshore flow that will gradually strengthen into \nSunday. Low level SW winds on Saturday will remain weak, allowing \nfor the east coast sea breeze to shift inland during the afternoon, \ncolliding with the west coast breeze across the interior. As \noffshore flow increases, the east coast sea breeze if it forms will \nlikely stall near to east of the I-95 corridor focusing boundary \ncollisions closer to the coast on Sunday. Moisture will continue to \nincrease and with sea breeze collisions focused across east central \nFlorida this will lead to a rise in shower and storm chances this \nweekend. PoPs increase to 30-50% for much of the area Saturday and \nup to 70% on Sunday. Deeper moisture (PW values > 2.0 inches) and \nweak steering flow will lead to an increased potential for locally \nheavy rainfall of 2-4 inches each day, which may result in mostly \nminor flooding. A Marginal Risk (5-14% chance) of excessive rainfall \nis in place across the area for Sunday when higher coverage of \nshowers and storms are forecast. Strong wind gusts and frequent \nlightning will also be possible with any stronger convection.\n\nHot and humid conditions continue this weekend, with highs in the \nlow to mid 90s. Current forecast has peak heat index values around \n102-107F for much of the area, but the added moisture may push heat \nindex values a little higher to 108-110F, which may require a Heat \nAdvisory for potions of the region. Widespread Moderate to Major \nHeatRisk, with localized Extreme HeatRisk conditions will continue \nto impact those without adequate cooling and hydration. Lows \ncontinue in the 70s. \n\nMonday-Thursday...(Modified Previous Discussion) Weak high \npressure ridging influence continues thru this period, though the \naxis remains south of central FL. A weak frontal boundary is \nforecast to drift south toward north FL near/past mid-week. \nOverall moisture remains fairly high thru the period surging \nnorthward mid-week along aforementioned front. Overall flow \noffshore forecast, with late day boundary collisions focused \nacross the eastern FL peninsula at least Mon and will see if the \nflow is too strong for the ECSB to even form Tue-Thu, though this \nremains far enough out with lower confidence just yet. Near to \nabove normal rain chances is forecast to continue with scattered \nto numerous showers and storms each afternoon and evening. Highs \ncontinue in the L-M90s with peak heat indices continuing to \naverage 102-107F each afternoon ahead of sea breeze/storms. Will \ncontinue to monitor for the need of any Heat Advisories, as \nportions of ECFL could still see heat index values reach around \n108-110F.",
